# Directions on the ferry town's plan: each one proved to lead to exactly one place.
lesson language-directions v=1 format=teach grade=4 unit=2 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "[[phrase.where-is-the-beach]]"
  goal "Follow and give directions in [[language.name]] on a town plan: left, right, straight on, first and second."

  look {
    scene 28x23 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        townplan plan places=["[[town.the-cafe]]", "[[town.the-shop]]", "[[town.the-park]]", "[[town.the-beach]]", "[[town.the-museum]]", "[[town.the-station]]", "[[town.the-square]]"]
      }
    }
    say """
      The child stands at the bottom of the plan. [[town.it-is-on-the-left]] is on the left and
      [[town.it-is-on-the-right]] is on the right. [[town.go-straight-on]] is straight on.
      [[town.the-first-on-the-left]] is the first street on the left, and
      [[town.the-second-on-the-right]] is the second street on the right. To ask the way, say
      [[phrase.where-is-the-beach]]
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.directions-name k=3
    }
    show language.directions-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show language.directions-meaning k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=5
    show language.directions-write k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.directions-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.directions-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.directions-write k=4 easy-k=2 hard-k=6
    show language.directions-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.directions-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.directions-write k=4
    }
  }
  language es {
    try stars=2 {
      show language.es-directions-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    }
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.directions-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[town.it-is-on-the-left]] On the left. [[town.it-is-on-the-right]] On the right. [[town.go-straight-on]] Straight on."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is left and right swapped, and counting a street past the one named.
    Every direction in the naming questions leads to exactly one building on the plan, so a wrong
    pick shows which step went wrong: the turn or the count. The three-star question has Ana facing
    the other way, where right and left swap, which is the hard part of directions in any language.
    From this grade the written words are marked with their accents: a word typed without its accent
    is not taken. The dictation is read from this sheet, where it prints as Read aloud, or played by
    the device. If the child is stuck, walk the directions in a room at home, turning left and right
    as you say them. Off the page, give directions in the language to someone hiding a toy. It meets
    England's key stage 2 languages programme (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7): "engage in
    conversations; ask and answer questions; express opinions and respond to those of others; seek
    clarification and help"; Japan's grades 5 and 6 foreign languages: "Can give instructions, make
    requests and respond to them by using basic expressions"; and CEFR A2, "Can understand sentences
    and frequently used expressions related to areas of most immediate relevance". The phrases are
    in [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[town.the-park]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[town.the-square]] and [[town.the-cafe]] and the words to write are [[town.left]] and [[town.right]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[town.the-beach]] and [[town.the-museum]] and the words to write are [[town.near]], [[town.far]] and [[town.second]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
